      "caseNotes": "GREENWAY PHARMACY — RECORD OF INTERVENTION\nPatient: Mr Liam Doyle\nAge: 27\nRegistered GP: Dr Marcus Bell, Greenway Health Centre\nAttended: 4 September 2026 (today), collecting salbutamol\nSeen in the consultation room after the counter assistant noticed the collection pattern.\n\nReason for contact:\nA pattern of reliever overuse identified from the dispensing record, together with poor inhaler technique demonstrated at the counter today.\n\nCurrent medications:\n- salbutamol 100 mcg inhaler — collected 26 July, 14 August and 4 September 2026, three inhalers in six weeks.\n- beclometasone 200 mcg inhaler, two puffs twice daily — last collected 8 May 2026, four months ago.\n- cetirizine 10 mg daily as required\n\nDispensing history:\nDuring 2025 salbutamol was collected roughly every three months.\nBeclometasone was collected regularly until the spring of 2026 and then not reordered.\nHe says he stopped the brown inhaler when his chest felt better in the spring and forgot to restart it.\nCetirizine is bought mainly in the summer and currently taken once or twice a week.\nPrescriptions are ordered through the practice app.\nNo repeat requests have been refused or delayed by the practice.\n\nThe concern:\nThree reliever inhalers in six weeks indicates use well above the accepted threshold, and the preventer has not been reordered in four months, so it cannot have been taken as prescribed. Both are markers of poorly controlled asthma and of increased risk of a severe attack.\n\nTechnique check today:\nActuated the inhaler before beginning to breathe in on two of three attempts. Breathed in quickly rather than slowly and steadily. Did not hold his breath afterwards. Does not use a spacer and says he has never been given one.\nCorrect technique was demonstrated. He repeated it correctly once, then slipped back on the next attempt.\nHe uses the same technique for both inhalers.\nBoth inhalers are within their expiry dates.\n\nSymptoms:\nWakes with cough or wheeze about twice a week. Uses the reliever before five-a-side football and again afterwards. Reports he has been reaching for it three or four times most days over the past fortnight.\nSymptoms are worse in cold air and when the warehouse is dusty.\nNo time off work because of his chest.\nSleep is otherwise good.\n\nRelevant history:\nAsthma since childhood. One attendance at the emergency department last winter, treated with oral steroids and discharged. No admission. No personal asthma action plan that he is aware of.\nHe was given a peak flow meter years ago but no longer has it.\nChildhood eczema, resolved by the age of ten.\nBroken collarbone playing football in 2018, healed.\nMild hay fever since his teens.\nNo hospital admissions for any reason.\n\nToday:\nMild expiratory wheeze audible at the counter. Speaking in full sentences. No accessory muscle use.\nPulse 84. Respiratory rate 16 at rest.\nComfortable and chatting throughout the consultation.\nColour normal.\n\nAllergies:\nNo known drug allergies.\n\nPrevious asthma care:\nLast asthma review at the practice was over two years ago, as far as he remembers.\nAfter the emergency attendance last winter he improved within a week of finishing the tablets.\nHe did not book the follow-up appointment suggested at the time, as he felt better.\n\nSocial:\nPlays five-a-side weekly. Recently changed jobs and now works in a warehouse. Non-smoker. Lives with his partner and a cat.\nThe cat has lived with them for five years; he has not noticed symptoms around it.\nHis partner is supportive and reminds him about appointments.\nThe warehouse job involves early starts. Drinks socially at weekends.\nLives in a ground-floor flat near the town centre and walks to work, about fifteen minutes each way.\nHis partner works shifts at the local hospital.\nEnjoys cooking and computer games. His mother also has asthma.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nAsked about a cream for mild acne. Enquired about hay-fever tablets for next spring.\nMentioned a routine dental check-up last month, all fine.\nAsked whether the pharmacy could text him when prescriptions are ready.\nAsked how to dispose of empty inhalers.\nAsked whether the pharmacy is open late on Thursdays.\n\nWhat he understands:\nHe knows the brown inhaler is the preventer, but did not know it should be used every day even when he feels well.\nHe agreed to restart it as prescribed and to attend an asthma review, and asked for an evening appointment if possible.\nHe wants to keep playing football and was relieved to hear that good control should make this easier.\nGiven a leaflet on inhaler technique to take home.",
      "recipient": "Dr Marcus Bell, General Practitioner, Greenway Health Centre",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write to the GP about repeated reliever overuse and poor inhaler technique, requesting an asthma review."
