      "caseNotes": "MEADOW PHARMACY — URGENT RECORD OF CONSULTATION\nPatient: Miss Hannah Reid\nAge: 22\nPrescriber: Dr Stephen Hollis, Meadow Neurology Service\nAttended: 4 September 2026 (today), without an appointment\nSeen in the consultation room at 9.20 am, accompanied by a friend from her course.\n\nReason for contact:\nPresented with a new and spreading rash that began during a lamotrigine dose increase. Urgent review requested.\n\nCurrent medications:\n- lamotrigine — increased from 25 mg to 50 mg daily on 27 August 2026, eight days ago.\n- sodium valproate 500 mg twice daily — unchanged since 2024\n- combined oral contraceptive — unchanged\n- paracetamol as required\n\nTitration history:\nLamotrigine started at 25 mg on alternate days on 30 July 2026, then 25 mg daily from 13 August 2026.\nNo rash or other side effect during those first four weeks.\nShe has the clinic's written dose schedule and has followed it exactly.\nNo new medicines, creams, washing powders or toiletries in the past month.\nDoses are taken each morning with breakfast, and she has never missed one.\nHer last clinic appointment was in July 2026, when the plan was agreed.\nValproate dose and tablets unchanged.\n\nThe concern:\nLamotrigine carries a risk of serious cutaneous reactions, and the risk is increased during titration and when it is combined with sodium valproate, which raises lamotrigine levels. The rash has appeared within the recognised window and is spreading. This requires same-day assessment rather than a routine appointment.\n\nThe rash:\nFirst noticed yesterday morning on the trunk. Now involves both upper arms and the front of the chest. Described as red, slightly raised and itchy. The patient reports it has spread noticeably since yesterday.\nShe has not applied anything to the rash.\nPhotographs she took yesterday evening and this morning show the spread.\nNo similar rash in the past.\nNo new pets, plants or travel in the past month.\nThe itch is worse in the evening and when she is warm; it disturbed her sleep last night.\nHer friend confirmed the timeline of the rash.\n\nAssessed today:\nNo involvement of the mouth, eyes or genital area reported. No fever reported and she feels well in herself. No facial swelling. No blistering seen or described. No sore throat.\nRash seen on the trunk, chest and upper arms, matching her account.\nLips, eyes and the inside of the mouth look normal.\n\nRelevant history:\nEpilepsy, diagnosed 2023. Generalised seizures, last seizure four months ago. Titration of lamotrigine ongoing with a further increase planned in two weeks.\nValproate Pregnancy Prevention Programme: no annual risk acknowledgement form seen on our record; contraception to be reviewed, as a combined pill is user-dependent and interacts with lamotrigine.\nChickenpox in childhood. Appendicectomy at the age of 14, uneventful.\nShe does not hold a driving licence.\nShe keeps a seizure diary, which shows no seizures since the spring.\n\nAdvice given today:\nAdvised not to take a further dose until she has been assessed, and to attend the emergency department immediately if she develops mouth or eye involvement, fever, blistering or facial swelling. She has been given this in writing.\nThe clinic line was telephoned at 9.45 am and a message left with the secretary for Dr Hollis.\nShe understood the advice, repeated it back correctly and agreed to follow it.\n\nAllergies:\nNo known drug allergies.\n\nPrevious reactions:\nNo previous rash or reaction to any medicine.\nMild hay fever in early summer, which needed no treatment this year.\n\nSocial:\nUniversity student, living in shared accommodation. Non-smoker.\nHer friend is staying with her tonight and can bring her back if needed.\nHer parents live two hours away and know she has been seen today.\nFinal-year biology student; exams begin in January.\nDrinks alcohol occasionally and has not done so since the dose increase.\nShares a kitchen and bathroom with four other students. Cooks for herself and eats regularly.\nWorks part time in the campus café at weekends.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nAsked about managing exam stress. Reports occasional migraines, unchanged for years.\nMentioned she is training for a charity run in the spring.\nAsked whether the pharmacy accepts a student discount card.\nAsked for somewhere quiet to wait while her friend fetched the car.\nMentioned that a second ear piercing last year healed well.\n\nFollow-up:\nShe has agreed to telephone the pharmacy if she has not heard from the clinic by 4 pm today.\nHer mobile number has been checked and recorded on the pharmacy record.",
