      "caseNotes": "OAKFIELD PHARMACY — RECORD OF CONSULTATION\nPatient: Mrs Carol Whitfield\nAge: 58\nRegistered GP: Dr Priya Nair, Oakfield Surgery\nAttended: 4 September 2026 (today)\nSeen in the consultation room at 2.30 pm.\n\nReason for contact:\nAttended reporting muscle pain that began after a recent increase in her statin dose. Clinical review requested.\n\nCurrent medications:\n- atorvastatin 40 mg at night — increased from 20 mg on 31 July 2026, five weeks ago.\n- amlodipine 5 mg daily\n- metformin 1 g twice daily\n- levothyroxine 75 mcg each morning\n\nMedication history:\nAll four medicines collected monthly on time; she uses a weekly pill organiser.\nLevothyroxine dose unchanged since 2020.\nMetformin taken with meals, with no stomach upset.\nNo over-the-counter medicines, herbal remedies or supplements at present.\nAtorvastatin is taken at about 10 pm each night.\nNo change of manufacturer for any of her medicines in recent months.\nShe reads the leaflet for every new pack and noticed muscle pain listed for the statin.\n\nThe symptoms:\nAching in both thighs and both shoulders for three weeks, and worsening. Described as a deep ache rather than cramp, present at rest and worse in the evenings. Symmetrical. No weakness reported and she can still climb stairs, though she has stopped her weekly exercise class because of the discomfort. Sleep is disturbed most nights.\nRates the ache 5 out of 10 in the evenings.\nApart from the class, she has kept to her usual routine at home and at work.\nWarm baths in the evening give brief relief.\nNo back pain and no joint swelling. The ache is the same on both sides.\n\nThe concern:\nThe onset follows a dose increase, the distribution is symmetrical and proximal, and there is no other obvious explanation. No recent unaccustomed exercise, no recent illness and no new medicine other than the dose change. This pattern is consistent with statin-associated muscle symptoms and warrants review and a creatine kinase measurement.\n\nRelevant history:\nNo previous statin intolerance; on atorvastatin 20 mg since 2021 without difficulty. Type 2 diabetes. Hypothyroidism, thyroid function checked in May 2026 and normal. eGFR normal at the last check four months ago.\nDiabetes diagnosed in 2019; diabetic eye screening in March 2026 showed no change.\nGall bladder removed in 2012.\nLast diabetes review in May 2026; she reports it went well.\nBunion surgery on the right foot in 2017, fully recovered. Two uncomplicated pregnancies.\n\nToday:\nNo dark urine reported. No fever. Able to mobilise unaided. Weight unchanged.\nBlood pressure 134/82. Pulse 70, regular.\nWeight 82 kg, the same as at her last visit.\nRose from the chair without using her arms. Walked normally across the room.\n\nAllergies:\nPenicillin — rash.\nThe rash was in childhood; she has avoided penicillin since.\n\nSocial:\nWorks part time in an office. Lives with her husband. Non-smoker.\nHer husband is supportive and has taken on the heavier housework for now.\nWalks the dog each morning, more slowly than usual.\nHer first grandchild was born in July; she visits most weekends.\nDrinks alcohol within recommended limits.\nHer office work is mostly seated and she has been able to keep working.\nEnjoys reading and baking. Her daughter lives nearby.\nShe and her husband are planning a holiday in Wales next spring.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nWishes to lose weight and asked about meal replacement products. Enquired about vitamin D. Reports a cat allergy, managed by avoidance.\nMentioned that her flu vaccination is booked at the surgery for October.\nAsked about the car park barrier, which was not working this morning.\nAsked whether the pharmacy offers a weight management service.\nMentioned that her dental check-up is due next month.\nCholesterol control:\nTotal cholesterol 5.8 mmol/L and non-HDL cholesterol 4.5 mmol/L in June 2026.\nNon-HDL had fallen by less than 40% from 5.6 mmol/L before treatment, which is why the dose was increased. No previous cardiovascular event; the statin is for primary prevention.\nThe previous lipid result, in 2025, was similar on the 20 mg dose.\nShe has made some changes to her diet this year.\n\nPatient's understanding:\nShe understands the statin is to lower her future heart risk and would like to stay on some form of cholesterol treatment if possible.\nShe is worried that the ache means lasting muscle damage and would welcome reassurance.\nShe has not stopped or changed the dose herself.\nShe is happy to have a blood test this week and can attend in the mornings.\n\nPrevious exercise:\nAttended the weekly class for two years before stopping three weeks ago.\nShe would like to return to it once the ache settles.",
