      "caseNotes": "RIVERSIDE PHARMACY — RECORD OF INTERVENTION\nPatient: Mr Colin Davies\nAge: 68\nRegistered GP: Dr Helen Pryce, Riverside Medical Centre\nAttended: 4 September 2026 (today)\nSeen in the consultation room; attended alone.\n\nReason for contact:\nPresented to collect a prescription from the out-of-hours service for an antibiotic that interacts significantly with his anticoagulant. Earlier monitoring and a possible dose adjustment are needed.\n\nPrescription presented:\nclarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for seven days, prescribed 3 September 2026 by the out-of-hours service for a chest infection.\nSeen by a doctor at the walk-in centre yesterday evening. No clinical notes came with the prescription.\nHe brought the paper prescription in himself. It is valid and correctly written.\nOut-of-hours service telephoned at 9.30 am; the duty doctor confirmed clarithromycin because of his penicillin allergy.\nThe duty doctor asked that the practice arrange an INR within three to five days of starting.\nClarithromycin supplied today after this discussion; he plans to take the first dose at lunchtime.\n\nCurrent regular medications:\nwarfarin 4 mg daily — atrial fibrillation, target INR 2.0 to 3.0\nbisoprolol 5 mg daily\natorvastatin 40 mg daily\nomeprazole 20 mg daily\n\nMedication routine:\nTakes warfarin at 6 pm each day, as one 3 mg and one 1 mg tablet.\nAll regular medicines collected monthly without gaps.\nOmeprazole taken since 2015 for reflux, well controlled.\nHas taken his warfarin at the same time each day for several years.\nUses a weekly pill organiser for the other medicines.\nHas not bought any over-the-counter medicine or herbal remedy recently.\n\nThe concern:\nClarithromycin is a potent enzyme inhibitor and markedly potentiates warfarin, and the rise in INR typically appears within a few days of starting. The risk of bleeding over a seven-day course is clinically significant. The patient was not told of the interaction and no earlier INR has been arranged.\nClarithromycin also interacts with atorvastatin; suggest withholding atorvastatin for the seven-day course.\nThe interaction was explained to the patient today in plain terms.\n\nAnticoagulation record:\nMost recent INR 2.6, taken on 14 August 2026, three weeks ago. Stable within range at every check for the past six months on the same dose. Next routine test due in four weeks.\nMonitored at the practice anticoagulation clinic. His record book was checked today and matches.\nNo missed doses recorded this year.\nOver the past year his readings have ranged from 2.2 to 2.9.\nWarfarin started in 2019 at diagnosis; no dose change for over a year.\nThe clinic runs on Tuesday and Thursday mornings.\n\nToday:\nNo bruising, no nosebleeds, no blood in the urine or stool. Reports a productive cough for four days, feels unwell and is keen to start the antibiotic without delay. Declined a suggestion to wait until the practice opens. Temperature 37.8 degrees. Blood pressure 130/78.\nGreen sputum, with no blood in it.\nPulse 76, irregular as expected. Breathing comfortably at rest.\nEating and drinking normally.\nAlert and talking in full sentences. Not short of breath at rest; no chest pain.\n\nRelevant history:\nAtrial fibrillation diagnosed 2019. Mild renal impairment, eGFR 58 in May 2026. No previous bleeding event.\nHernia repair in 2008, uneventful.\nEarly cataract on the right, awaiting a routine appointment.\nFlu vaccination last given in October 2025.\nOne episode of gout in 2016, no recurrence. Appendix removed as a child.\n\nAllergies:\nPenicillin — rash.\nRash with amoxicillin in 2005; no breathing problems at the time.\n\nSocial:\nLives alone. Reliable with medication and keeps his own anticoagulation record book. Ex-smoker, stopped 2010.\nHis daughter lives ten minutes away, visits twice a week and can drive him to the clinic.\nRetired bus driver; volunteers at the community garden.\nDrinks one or two units on Sundays only. Has a mobile phone and can receive text reminders.\nLives in a bungalow with a small garden. Cooks for himself; his diet has not changed recently.\nEnjoys bowls at the local club in the summer. His neighbour checks on him most days.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nAsked about a cough medicine to buy. Enquired whether he is due a pneumococcal vaccination.\nAsked whether his next monthly prescription could be delivered.\nAsked about opening hours over the next bank holiday.\nMentioned that his new hearing aids are working well.\nAsked whether the pharmacy recycles old blister packs.\n\nPatient's understanding:\nHe knows warfarin thins the blood and says he will report any bleeding, dark stools or unusual bruising.\nHe is willing to attend for an earlier blood test and would prefer a morning appointment.\nHe keeps his record book with him and will bring it to the next test.\n\nInfection history:\nOne chest infection in 2023, treated by the practice; recovered fully without admission.\nNo other antibiotic courses in the past two years.",
